# Hook

<So I decided I was going to cook hibachi.
I’ve got the chicken out. The vegetables. The rice.
I roll an egg across the griddle and say, to absolutely nobody,
“There’s my eggroll.”
I stack up some onions. Make my little volcano.
Pour some oil in it. Try to light it.
Nothing.
Draw a little smiley face with the oil. Try that.
Nothing.
But the griddle’s hot. I put the chicken down.
It sizzles. I squirt some teriyaki on it.
Now we’re getting somewhere.
I let it cook, get my spatula underneath it.
I’m about to make this flip look good.
It doesn’t move.
I push a little harder.
The top half moves.
The bottom half has become one with the Blackstone.
“Hey, babe! We’re having shredded chicken tonight.”
Then come the vegetables.
Some of them fry. Some of them attach themselves permanently.
So now, instead of flipping, I’m scraping.
Tessa opens the door.
“What’s that sound?”
“That’s just Hibachi Kyle at work.”
The rice wasn’t any better.
It just accepted that it lived there now.
My spatula’s going across the top.
There’s a whole layer underneath that’s not coming with us.
I don’t even know why I tried the eggs.
By the time I finished, they were a breakfast memorial.
Nothing flipped through the air.
Nothing danced across the griddle.
There wasn’t any choo-choo train.
Just me. Scraping dinner.
But I told myself, “It’s probably not that bad.”
Boy, was I wrong.
I bring it inside.
Tessa, bless her heart, says it’s good.
She loves me.
Then she asks, “Did you season it?”
“I seasoned the rice.”
I learned something about that griddle. 
Apparently, you can’t just take it out of the box,
put it together,
and start throwing chicken at it.
It needs seasoned.
Heat. Oil. Smoke.
Then you do it again.
That process builds a layer on the surface.
And that layer has a job to do.
I had it purchased.
I had it assembled.
I had dinner planned.
But I had skipped the preparation.
